Let $h$ be the length of the altitude from $A$ to $\overline{BC}.$  Then
\[\tan A = \frac{h}{BC} = \frac{h}{15}.\]To maximize $\tan A,$ we maximize $h.$

[asy]
unitsize (0.5 cm);

pair A, B, C, D;

A = (4,8);
C = (0,0);
B = (8,0);
D = (A + reflect(B,C)*(A))/2;

draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(A--D);

label("$A$", A, N);
label("$B$", B, S);
label("$C$", C, SW);
label("$D$", D, S);
[/asy]

Since $D$ varies along $\overline{BC}$ as shown, the maximum value of $h$ occurs when $D = C,$ in which case $h = AD.$

By the Triangle Inequality, $AC < AB + BC = 35,$ so $h = AD < 35.$  Hence, the maximum value of $h$ is 35, so the largest possible value of $\tan A$ is $\frac{35}{15} = \boxed{\frac{7}{3}}.$</think> <answer>\frac{7}{3}</answer>

