First, we can assume that $\theta_1 > \theta_2,$ so $\theta_1 = \theta_2 + \frac{\pi}{2}.$

[asy]
unitsize(0.3 cm);

pair A, B, O;

theta1 = 7*pi/12;
theta2 = theta1 - pi/2;
A = (12*cos(theta1),12*sin(theta1));
B = (12*cos(theta2),12*sin(theta2));
O = (0,0);

draw((-15,0)--(15,0));
draw((0,-15)--(0,15));
draw((-15,0)--O--(15,0));
draw((0,-15)--O--(15,0));
draw(A--B);

label("$O$", O, SW);
dot("$A$", A, N);
dot("$B$", B, SE);
[/asy]

We can assume that $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius 12 (we can do this by scaling the figure).  (We will show that this does not affect the distance between $A$ and $B$.)

In polar coordinates, the distance between two points $(r_1, \theta_1)$ and $(r_2, \theta_2)$ is
\[\sqrt{r_1^2 + r_2^2 - 2r_1 r_2 \cos (\theta_1 - \theta_2)},\]so the distance between $A$ and $B$ is
\begin{align*}
AB &= \sqrt{12^2 + 12^2 - 2 \cdot 12 \cdot 12 \cos \frac{\pi}{2}} \\
&= \sqrt{2 \cdot 12^2} = \boxed{12 \sqrt{2}}.
\end{align*}</think> <answer>12 \sqrt{2}</answer>

